    "q-213257": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213257",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267734/assets/d530d373a72ad178638d7701acb0ffb13d8427b5a0cb07dc4403d2b50ac7be45.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{-1,0,1\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(0,1)$\n\nII. $(1,0)$\n\nIII. $(1,1)$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(0,1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $0$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(1,0)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $1$ and ends at the node $0.$\n\n- The pair $(1,1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have a loop from the node $1$ to itself.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
