    "q-213263": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213263",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267734/assets/3e7f57af0023e3949f76b9bd648097dde66d623752f88b8015cbf509c719b8d3.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{△,◯,⋆\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(◯,⋆)$\n\nII. $(△,⋆)$\n\nIII. $(△,△)$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(◯,⋆)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $◯$ and ends at the node $⋆.$\n\n- The pair $(△,⋆)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $△$ and ends at the node $⋆.$\n\n- The pair $(△,△)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $△$ to itself.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
